               <p xml:id="idm167">Marmorkirken (The Marble Church), the official name being Frederiks Kirke (Frederik's Church), was designed by the architect Nicolai Eigtved in 1740. The foundation stone was laid in 1749, but the construction slowed down due to budget cuts and the death of Eigtved in 1754. In 1770 the original plans for the church were abandoned by Johann Friedrich Struensee. The church was left incomplete and stood as a ruin for nearly 150 years. In 1874 the ruins of the uncompleted church and the church square was sold to the financier and industrialist C.F. Tietgen who got Ferdinand Mehldahl to design the church in its final form and financed its construction. Due to financial restrictions, the original plans for the church to be build almost entirely from marble were discarded, and instead Meldahl used limestone. The church finally opened to the public on 19 August 1894.</p>
            </history>
